[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **single‑file `.pmtiles` archive** into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`) that makes a `pmtiles://` URL work with MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why “once”?**  
> `addProtocol` registers a global handler. Registering it inside a React component’s render loop or inside a `new maplibregl.Map()` call will create duplicate handlers and can leak memory.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – `pmtilesProtocol.tile` already contains the compatibility shim that works with the new promise‑based handler signature, so you don’t have to write any `async (params, abort)` code yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### Vector tiles (most common)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // <-- the PMTiles source
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The protocol prefix tells MapLibre to use the handler we just registered
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        // No need to list `tiles: [...]` – the protocol reads the archive header
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the "water" layer that lives inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the .pmtiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example: draw roads from the same source
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### Raster tiles (imagery)

```js
{
  // inside the `style.sources` object
  satellite: {
    type: 'raster',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/sat.pmtiles',
    tileSize: 256               // optional – defaults to 256
  }
},
{
  // inside the `style.layers` array
  id: 'satellite',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'satellite',
  paint: {}
}
```

### Raster‑DEM (terrain)

```js
{
  elevation: {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation/elev.pmtiles',
    encoding: 'terrarium'      // or "mapbox"
  }
},
// Enable 3‑D terrain in the map
map.setTerrain({ source: 'elevation', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

---

## 4️⃣ Hosting requirements for the `.pmtiles` file

| Requirement | What to check |
|-------------|---------------|
| **HTTP Range support** | The server must honor `Range: bytes=` headers. All S3‑compatible stores (AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, Wasabi, etc.) do this out of the box. |
| **CORS** | The response must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) **and** `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. Otherwise the browser blocks the range request. |
| **Cache‑Control** | Because the archive is immutable, set a long max‑age, e.g. `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000`. CDNs will cache the range responses automatically. |
| **File size limits** | GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc. often cap static files at 100 MiB. For larger archives use S3/R2 or split the data into several region‑based `.pmtiles` files. |

---

## 5️⃣ Quick sanity‑check checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| `npm i pmtiles` installed |
| `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile)` executed **before** `new maplibregl.Map()` |
| Source uses **`url:`** (not `tiles:`) and the URL is prefixed with `pmtiles://` |
| The archive’s CORS headers allow your domain and the `Range` header |
| The host returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes` |
| Layer’s `"source-layer"` matches a layer name inside the archive (run `pmtiles show my.pmtiles` to list them) |
| For raster‑DEM, `encoding` is set (`terrarium` or `mapbox`) and `map.setTerrain()` is called |

If any of those fail you’ll see either **blank tiles** (vector) or **404/0‑byte responses** (raster). Open the Network tab, look for a request to `...pmtiles` with a `Range:` header, and verify the response contains `Content-Range:` and the tile data.

---

## 6️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Build the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, and you should see the vector data rendered from the single `.pmtiles` file.

