## 7️⃣ TL;DR – One‑liner summary

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);

new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      myData: { type: 'vector', url: 'pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/mydata.pmtiles' }
    },
    layers: [{ id: 'myLayer', type: 'fill', source: 'myData', 'source-layer': 'my_layer', paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff0' } }]
  }
});
```

That’s it—your `.pmtiles` archive is now a first‑class tile source, with no tile server required. Happy mapping!

## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback‑style** handler:

```js
// v3 (and earlier)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise‑based** and the handler must return an **object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
The second argument is now an **`AbortController`** (instead of a callback) and the handler is allowed to be `async`.

If you keep the old callback‑style code, MapLibre receives a resolved value that has **no `data` field**, so it treats the tile as “empty” → you see blank tiles (or a silent failure).

---

## Fixing the handler

### 1. Convert the callback to an async function

```js
// ✅ v4+ – async handler that returns {data}
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // `params.url` is the full URL that the protocol should fetch.
  // The AbortController lets the request be cancelled if the map no longer needs the tile.
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the request and makes MapLibre report an error for this tile.
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // Most tile sources (vector, raster, raster‑dem) expect raw bytes.
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // The contract: return an object with a `data` key.
  // Optional: `cacheControl` and `expires` can be added if you want to control HTTP‑caching.
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

### 2. If you need to respect the abort signal

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

When the map discards a tile (e.g., you pan away), the `abortController.signal` will fire and `fetch` will reject with an `AbortError`. That is fine – MapLibre just drops the request.

### 3. Optional cache‑control headers

If your static host does **not** send proper `Cache‑Control`/`Expires` headers, you can supply them yourself:

```js
return {
  data: buffer,
  cacheControl: 'public, max-age=31536000', // 1 year
  expires: new Date(Date.now() + 365 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000).toUTCString()
};
```

MapLibre will forward those values to the browser’s cache.

### 4. Register the protocol **once**, before any map is created

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Example: keep the pmtiles shim (works for v3‑v6)
const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);   // <-- works on all versions

// Register your custom protocol
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  const resp = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Tile error ${resp.status}`);
  const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buf };
});
```

> **Tip:** If you are using a framework (React, Vue, etc.) put the registration in a top‑level module or a `useEffect(() => { … }, [])` that runs only once. Registering inside a component that mounts/unmounts repeatedly can lead to duplicate handlers or memory leaks.

### 5. Clean‑up (optional but recommended)

When the last map on the page is destroyed you can remove the protocol:

```js
map.on('remove', () => {
  maplibregl.removeProtocol('my');
});
```

---

## Full minimal example (vector tiles stored in a PMTiles file)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre v4 + PMTiles</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // ---- 1️⃣ Register the PMTiles protocol (shim works for all versions) ----
  const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);

  // ---- 2️⃣ (Optional) Register a custom protocol that you wrote yourself ----
  // maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abort) => {
  //   const r = await fetch(params.url, {signal: abort.signal});
  //   if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`Tile fetch ${r.status}`);
  //   const buf = await r.arrayBuffer();
  //   return {data: buf};
  // });

  // ---- 3️⃣ Create the map -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Note: use `url:` (not `tiles:`) so the PMTiles header supplies min/max zoom.
        osm: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://YOUR_BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/planet_osm.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // ---- 4️⃣ Clean‑up (optional) --------------------------------------------
  map.on('remove', () => {
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
    // maplibregl.removeProtocol('my');   // if you added a custom one
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://YOUR_BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/planet_osm.pmtiles` with the URL of your own PMTiles file (any static host that supports **Range** requests and CORS).*

---

## Quick checklist for the upgrade

| ✅ What you need to change | ❌ What you must **remove** |
|---------------------------|----------------------------|
| Use an **async** (or promise‑returning) function as the handler. | The old `(params, callback)` signature. |
| Return **`{ data: <ArrayBuffer|Uint8Array|Blob> }`** (or add `cacheControl`/`expires`). | Return the raw buffer directly. |
| Second argument is an **`AbortController`** (use `signal` in `fetch`). | Ignoring the abort signal is fine, but don’t treat the argument as a callback. |
| Register the protocol **once** before any `new maplibregl.Map()`. | Register inside a component that may mount multiple times. |
| If you use the official `pmtiles` library, keep the single line `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile)`. | Trying to wrap the library yourself with a callback‑style shim. |

---

## Why the `pmtiles.Protocol().tile` shim still works

The `pmtiles` NPM package (≥ 3.0.0) ships a **compatibility wrapper** called `v3compat`. Its `protocol.tile` method detects whether the second argument is a callback **or** an `AbortController` and adapts accordingly. Therefore the one‑liner:

```js
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

continues to work on MapLibre v3, v4, v5, and v6 without any code change. If you are on an older `pmtiles` (< 3.0.0) you must upgrade the library; otherwise the old callback‑only implementation will break exactly as you observed.

---

## TL;DR – What to paste into your project

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Register PMTiles protocol (shim works on all GL versions)
const pm = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pm.tile);

// If you have your own custom protocol, rewrite it like this:
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abort) => {
  const resp = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abort.signal });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Tile fetch ${resp.status}`);
  const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buf };
});
```

Now your map will load tiles again after the v4 upgrade. 🎉
